      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Concepts worth knowing first</h2>
  <p>
    Four ideas explain most of the interface, and getting them straight avoids a lot of confusion
    later.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Application</strong></td>
        <td>A product. It holds no platform detail of its own — that lives on its identifiers.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Identifier</strong></td>
        <td>
          What the app is called on one platform: an iOS bundle ID, an Android package name, a web
          domain. Build profiles and credentials attach here, not to the app.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Build</strong></td>
        <td>
          One build attempt, with a <em>reporting mode</em> that decides who owns its status:
          your runner (manual), a CI provider (automatic), or Expo (EAS).
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Integration</strong></td>
        <td>
          A connection to something outside: GitHub, GitLab, Expo, an HTTP endpoint, a chat
          channel. Pipelines, webhooks, and notification destinations all hang off one.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What only the console can do</h2>
  <p>
    Most screens have an API and CLI equivalent, but a few actions exist here alone. Knowing which
    saves you looking for a command that was never written:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Connecting GitHub or GitLab.</strong> The flow is OAuth and needs a browser. Once
      connected, the CLI can read and use the integration.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Notification destinations.</strong> Slack, Discord, and Telegram routing has no API
      at all — see <a href="/platform/integrations/notifications">Notifications</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Team membership and roles.</strong> Invitations and role changes are console-only.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Revealing a stored secret.</strong> Secure fields can be read in the vault UI; the
      API exposes them only under an explicit scope.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where to start</h2>
  <p>
    If you are setting a workspace up for the first time, this order avoids rework: create the
    <a href="/platform/applications">application and its identifiers</a>, add
    <a href="/platform/credentials-vault">signing material</a>, connect a
    <a href="/platform/integrations">CI/CD provider</a> and define a pipeline, then issue a
    scoped <a href="/platform/api-keys">API key</a> for the pipeline to report with.
  </p>
  <p>
    Installing the platform itself is a separate topic and is not covered here.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
